A dataset card is only useful if it answers two questions fast: “Can I trust this data?” and “Can I use it without breaking things?” We’ve burned through hours debugging mismatches because a card omitted schema changes or data drift. The best ones include clear provenance, versioning, and usage examples with actual code—no fluff. Bonus points if they flag known biases early. Otherwise, it’s just documentation theater.  

(Also, if your team uses CI/CD, make sure the card updates automatically with each dataset release—manual updates are a one-way ticket to inconsistency.)

